module examples.layout_runtime_const_len

# plans/M10.md item A2b, decision 581: **this is 03-hardware.md §3.1's own
# example, verbatim** — minus the `@placed static` half, which needs a
# `static` declaration this revision of the language does not have — and it
# now *compiles*. Item A2 pinned this same file as a rejection
# (`golden/err-layout-runtime-const-len`, decision 580: an array length was an
# integer literal and nothing else); this case is that file, renamed and
# flipped, because the compiler agreeing with the doc is the item's whole
# point.
#
# How the length is resolved, and where:
#
# - `sema::types::check_layouts` still runs *before name resolution* and still
#   evaluates nothing. Meeting `[TurnArea; N_TURNS]` it **defers**
#   `TurnTable`: it checks every rule that does not need a byte count (kind,
#   `endian=`, the element type, `@offset` shape, the nesting and cycle rules)
#   and reports no size at all rather than a wrong one. Decision 580's
#   reasoning is intact — no second name resolver exists in that pass.
# - `sema::types::complete_layouts` runs after `eval::check_comptime`, when
#   `N_TURNS` has been type-checked and evaluated by the one real evaluator,
#   and re-lays `TurnTable` out with the length known. Every size-dependent
#   rule — overlap, ordering, alignment, implicit padding, total bytes — is
#   applied there, to the completed table.
#
# `@offset(n)` did **not** move with it: it is still an integer literal, for
# decision 580's own reason. Only lengths moved, because only lengths are what
# M10's per-image tables (`n_turns`, `reply_ring_capacity`) need.
#
# The reported bytes are the ones `golden/check-layout-runtime` pins for the
# literal `4`: `turns` is 32 bytes at 0x8, because an array's alignment is its
# element's (4), not its own size.

const N_TURNS: u32 = 4


@layout(runtime, endian=little)
struct TurnArea:
    state: u32
    waiter: u32


@layout(runtime, endian=little)
struct TurnTable:
    rr_cursor: u64
    turns: [TurnArea; N_TURNS]
